WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.419
Hi, I'm Nutmeg!

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:05.053
Tinfield 1 carries a fifty-one-billion-parameter hash table.

00:00:05.053 --> 00:00:09.193
Here's what it is, and why a token barely touches it.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:15.237
Tinfield 1 is Qwen3.8-Flash-Next with its attention retrained: one point eight five

00:00:15.237 --> 00:00:16.866
percent of the weights.

00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:20.123
Its biggest piece is a hashed n-gram table.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.405
A token climbs through forty-eight layers of attention and a mixture of

00:00:25.405 --> 00:00:27.032
five hundred and twelve experts.

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:30.713
The fine-tune moved every attention projection, and nothing else.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:34.741
All five hundred and twelve experts, in all forty-eight layers, are byte

00:00:34.741 --> 00:00:36.244
for byte the base model.

00:00:36.664 --> 00:00:40.694
And at layer two, one extra input arrives: a row from the

00:00:40.694 --> 00:00:43.380
engram table, which is Qwen's, and also unchanged.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.423
Take the last two or three token ids, and hash them with

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:50.234
a polynomial of three large multipliers.

00:00:50.654 --> 00:00:56.137
Reduce that hash modulo sixteen different primes, each just above twenty million.

00:00:56.137 --> 00:00:58.879
Sixteen heads, sixteen unrelated collision patterns.

00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:02.342
Each head picks one row of a hundred and sixty numbers from

00:01:02.342 --> 00:01:03.864
its own slice of the table.

00:01:04.284 --> 00:01:08.401
At layer two, one extra input arrives: a row from the engram

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.460
table, which is Qwen's, and unchanged.

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:15.112
With one huge table, two n-grams that land on the same

00:01:15.112 --> 00:01:16.341
row are confused forever.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.220
With sixteen prime-sized heads, a collision under one prime almost never repeats

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.822
under another, so the noise averages out.

00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:30.047
A token reads two thousand five hundred and sixty values out of fifty-one billion.

00:01:30.047 --> 00:01:32.587
About five kilobytes, and no matrix multiply.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.933
So embeddings scale by lookup, not by matrix multiply.

00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:40.802
That's why the table can stay memory-mapped on the CPU side.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:47.080
To recap: retrained attention, and a hashed n-gram table read through sixteen primes.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.618
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:49.618 --> 00:01:50.223
I'm Nutmeg.

00:01:50.223 --> 00:01:50.526
Bye!
